TI  - Fish-specific piscidins MsPiscidin1 and MsPiscidin3 enhance antiviral defense through immunomodulation in largemouth bass (Micropterus salmoides)
PY  - 2027
T2  - Aquaculture
VL  - 627
C7  - 744485
DO  - 10.1016/j.aquaculture.2026.744485
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048207290?origin=resultslist
AB  - Antimicrobial peptides (AMPs) are important components of teleost innate immunity, yet the antiviral mechanisms of fish-specific piscidins remain incompletely understood, particularly with respect to host-directed immunomodulatory activity. Here, we investigated the indirect antiviral functions of two largemouth bass piscidins, MsPiscidin1 and MsPiscidin3, against Micropterus salmoides rhabdovirus (MSRV). Using a Micropterus salmoides dorsal fin (MSDF) cell model, we found that pre-treatment with either peptide markedly reduced MSRV-induced cytopathic effects and viral glycoprotein (G) gene expression. Although peptide treatment alone did not induce basal expression of antiviral genes, it established a poised antiviral state that enabled earlier and stronger induction of IFN-I, ISG15, Viperin, and Mx following viral challenge. Comparative phosphoproteomic analysis revealed substantial overlap in the phosphoprotein profiles induced by MsPiscidin1 and MsPiscidin3, with enriched signatures associated with membrane remodeling, autophagy, vesicle trafficking, PI3K-mTOR signaling, ubiquitin-dependent regulation, and innate immune-related pathways. Consistent with these changes, peptide-primed cells exhibited earlier TANK-binding kinase 1 (TBK1) phosphorylation after MSRV infection, supporting more rapid activation of antiviral signaling. In vivo, peptide pre-treatment conferred detectable protection in juvenile largemouth bass in response to subsequent viral infection. Collectively, these findings indicate that MsPiscidin1 and MsPiscidin3 enhance antiviral defense primarily through immunomodulation rather than direct virucidal activity, thereby expanding current understanding of functional diversification among teleost piscidins in antiviral immunity. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
